# Roast Potatoes

Status: Untested

## Recipe Name:
Roast Potatoes

## Servings:
4 to 6 as a side (estimate, the video does not state a yield)

## Prep Time:
15 minutes

## Cook Time:
About 1 hour 30 minutes (20 minutes parboil, 10 minutes steam, 1 hour roast)

## Ingredients:
- 2 kg Maris Piper potatoes
- Maldon sea salt
- Duck fat, a generous amount (beef dripping or olive oil also work; duck fat gives the best result)
- 1 bulb garlic, halved across the middle
- 1 bunch thyme
- Chopped rosemary, optional, if you are serving pork

Four things decide this dish: the right potato, a sharp knife, a heavy pan, and plenty of fat. Maris Piper is the pick. Cut the pieces to an even size and not too small, with a clean, smooth face. Peel top to bottom and wash the potatoes before peeling, not after. Do not boil them until they fall apart, or they will collapse when you shake them and you lose the fluffy edges that crisp. Get the roasting tray hot in the oven, not on the hob, so the pan itself starts the browning.

## Instructions:
1. Heat the oven to 190 °C fan. Put a heavy roasting tray with a lot of duck fat in it into the oven. It stays there as long as the potatoes take to parboil.
2. Peel the potatoes top to bottom. Rinse them before peeling only. Cut them into even pieces, not too small, with a very sharp knife. Keep each face smooth and flat.
3. Put the potatoes in a sturdy pan and cover with cold water. Add sea salt. Bring to a boil.
4. Parboil until a knife goes into a potato with slight resistance but not through, and the potato still holds its shape. About 20 minutes from cold water, or 8 to 10 minutes of boiling in the written recipe. Stop there; do not cook them soft.
5. Drain into a colander and shake them to rough up the edges. Cover with a cloth and leave for 10 minutes so the steam escapes and the surface dries.
6. Shake the colander again, hard, until the outsides go fluffy. Put back any pieces that break off.
7. Take the hot tray from the oven. Add the halved garlic and the thyme to the fat, then the potatoes. Turn them so every piece is coated. Keep them in one layer with room to move, and add a little salt. They are already rich, so go easy.
8. Roast at 190 °C fan for 30 minutes. Turn every potato, then roast at 180 °C fan for a further 30 minutes, until deep golden and crisp. Turning often after that is fine.
9. Discard the garlic. Finish with chopped thyme and a little salt. Serve at once; they lose their crunch as they cool.

